WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.215
Lesson 1.8.

00:00:02.215 --> 00:00:04.560
Privacy and physical security.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.765
By the end, you'll know how an address

00:00:06.765 --> 00:00:09.084
gets linked to your identity, and how to

00:00:09.084 --> 00:00:11.572
structure your keys so no single person can

00:00:11.572 --> 00:00:13.495
ever be forced to move everything.

00:00:14.795 --> 00:00:16.085
Here's the plan.

00:00:16.085 --> 00:00:18.666
First, why a public ledger means privacy

00:00:18.666 --> 00:00:21.610
is something you have to actively protect.

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:24.467
Second, the real physical risk this creates, and

00:00:24.467 --> 00:00:27.275
the one structural fix that actually works.

00:00:27.275 --> 00:00:29.697
And third, the quieter habits, at home and

00:00:29.697 --> 00:00:31.555
on your devices, that back it up.

00:00:32.855 --> 00:00:35.862
Every earlier lesson in this module protected

00:00:35.862 --> 00:00:38.215
you from losing funds to a mistake,

00:00:38.215 --> 00:00:40.215
a scam, or a bad contract.

00:00:40.215 --> 00:00:41.790
This one is different.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:44.202
It protects you from what happens when other

00:00:44.202 --> 00:00:45.970
people can see how much you have.

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:48.330
A public ledger.

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:51.225
Start with the trade-off.

00:00:51.225 --> 00:00:54.253
The same public ledger that lets anyone verify a

00:00:54.253 --> 00:00:57.528
transaction also lets anyone who links an address to

00:00:57.528 --> 00:01:00.115
you see its full balance and history.

00:01:00.115 --> 00:01:01.940
Not just today's balance.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:04.132
Every transaction it has ever made, and

00:01:04.132 --> 00:01:05.830
every one it makes from now on.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:09.545
Here's how that link actually gets made.

00:01:09.545 --> 00:01:11.620
Say you withdraw from an exchange.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.469
That exchange already knows who you are, because

00:01:14.469 --> 00:01:17.095
it collected your ID when you signed up.

00:01:17.095 --> 00:01:19.065
The moment you withdraw, it records

00:01:19.065 --> 00:01:21.171
which address received the funds.

00:01:21.171 --> 00:01:25.203
Separately, maybe you register an easy-to-remember name for that

00:01:25.203 --> 00:01:28.242
same address, or you post it publicly asking for

00:01:28.242 --> 00:01:30.651
a donation or showing off a trade.

00:01:30.651 --> 00:01:32.223
Any one of those, on its own,

00:01:32.223 --> 00:01:34.356
connects your name to that address.

00:01:34.356 --> 00:01:37.703
And once it's connected, every future transaction from

00:01:37.703 --> 00:01:40.016
that address is now readable as yours.

00:01:41.316 --> 00:01:42.406
Quick check.

00:01:42.406 --> 00:01:43.929
What does linking an address

00:01:43.929 --> 00:01:45.661
to your identity actually reveal?

00:01:50.081 --> 00:01:52.415
The answer: its full balance and history,

00:01:52.415 --> 00:01:54.637
both what's there now and everything it

00:01:54.637 --> 00:01:55.971
does from that point on.

00:01:57.271 --> 00:01:59.202
And it isn't just other individuals

00:01:59.202 --> 00:02:00.736
who can make that link.

00:02:00.736 --> 00:02:04.556
Chain-analysis firms exist specifically to cluster addresses

00:02:04.556 --> 00:02:06.748
and attach names to them, and they

00:02:06.748 --> 00:02:10.116
sell that service to exchanges and investigators.

00:02:10.116 --> 00:02:13.201
None of this requires anyone to break in anywhere.

00:02:13.201 --> 00:02:15.024
It's all sitting on a ledger that

00:02:15.024 --> 00:02:16.901
was built to be readable by design.

00:02:18.201 --> 00:02:19.056
Link less.

00:02:20.356 --> 00:02:22.881
So the habit is simple: link less.

00:02:22.881 --> 00:02:26.421
Don't post addresses, screenshots, or wins publicly.

00:02:26.421 --> 00:02:30.088
Keep a separate address for anything public, a donation

00:02:30.088 --> 00:02:32.970
link, an NFT you show off, and a completely

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:36.151
different one for savings you never mention.

00:02:36.151 --> 00:02:39.844
And remember that any exchange withdrawal already links

00:02:39.844 --> 00:02:42.811
your ID to whichever address receives it, so

00:02:42.811 --> 00:02:45.976
treat that receiving address as semi-public too.

00:02:47.276 --> 00:02:50.891
Here's the difference this makes, as one real comparison.

00:02:50.891 --> 00:02:52.893
Alex posts a screenshot of a big

00:02:52.893 --> 00:02:55.316
win, address visible in the corner.

00:02:55.316 --> 00:02:58.369
Anyone can now see Alex's balance, and follow

00:02:58.369 --> 00:03:01.656
every future move: a target has been created.

00:03:01.656 --> 00:03:04.594
The fix costs nothing: no screenshots, a vault

00:03:04.594 --> 00:03:07.470
address that has never once been shared, and,

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:09.783
as you'll see next, a multisig so no

00:03:09.783 --> 00:03:11.221
single key can move it.

00:03:12.521 --> 00:03:14.076
Put a rough shape on it.

00:03:14.076 --> 00:03:16.920
Across real cases of people getting doxxed

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:20.557
on-chain, exchange withdrawals are usually the biggest

00:03:20.557 --> 00:03:23.436
source, because ID is already attached.

00:03:23.436 --> 00:03:26.591
Public posts and screenshots are the second biggest, and

00:03:26.591 --> 00:03:30.156
they're the one source that's entirely within your control.

00:03:30.156 --> 00:03:33.701
Name services and on-chain analysis make up the rest.

00:03:35.001 --> 00:03:38.131
One more habit worth naming: address reuse.

00:03:38.131 --> 00:03:41.376
Using the same address everywhere, for every swap,

00:03:41.376 --> 00:03:44.494
every deposit, every payment, makes every one of

00:03:44.494 --> 00:03:47.466
those chain-analysis links easier to draw.

00:03:47.466 --> 00:03:50.039
A fresh address for anything that doesn't

00:03:50.039 --> 00:03:52.611
need to persist costs nothing and quietly

00:03:52.611 --> 00:03:54.326
removes most of the pattern.

00:03:55.626 --> 00:03:56.611
Physical risk.

00:03:57.911 --> 00:03:59.909
Here's the risk that makes this lesson

00:03:59.909 --> 00:04:02.156
different from the rest of the module.

00:04:02.156 --> 00:04:06.404
Criminals have specifically targeted people known to hold crypto,

00:04:06.404 --> 00:04:10.116
in person, to coerce them into signing a transaction.

00:04:10.116 --> 00:04:12.714
In this industry it's bluntly called a wrench

00:04:12.714 --> 00:04:15.199
attack, and it's real enough that it shapes

00:04:15.199 --> 00:04:17.514
how serious holders structure their keys.

00:04:18.814 --> 00:04:21.263
Low profile is the first defence, and

00:04:21.263 --> 00:04:24.099
it's just everything from the last section:

00:04:24.099 --> 00:04:27.299
no posts, no screenshots, no visible balance.

00:04:27.299 --> 00:04:29.294
But a low profile can fail.

00:04:29.294 --> 00:04:30.609
Someone talks.

00:04:30.609 --> 00:04:31.924
Someone guesses.

00:04:31.924 --> 00:04:33.806
So the real defence has to work

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:36.099
even if you've already been identified.

00:04:37.399 --> 00:04:39.644
That real fix is a multisig with

00:04:39.644 --> 00:04:42.734
keys held in separate physical locations.

00:04:42.734 --> 00:04:44.414
Say it's set up two of three.

00:04:44.414 --> 00:04:46.039
One key lives with you.

00:04:46.039 --> 00:04:48.484
One lives with a family member, or in

00:04:48.484 --> 00:04:51.614
a safe deposit box, somewhere else entirely.

00:04:51.614 --> 00:04:53.027
One lives with a lawyer

00:04:53.027 --> 00:04:54.799
or a trusted third party.

00:04:54.799 --> 00:04:57.188
No single key can move funds alone; any

00:04:57.188 --> 00:04:59.339
two of the three have to agree.

00:04:59.339 --> 00:05:02.532
Which means no single person, including you, under

00:05:02.532 --> 00:05:05.524
coercion, can be forced to move everything.

00:05:05.524 --> 00:05:08.314
The other key holders simply aren't there to sign.

00:05:09.613 --> 00:05:12.251
Setting this up is a few concrete decisions,

00:05:12.251 --> 00:05:14.603
not new software you have to learn.

00:05:14.603 --> 00:05:17.989
First, pick three people or places that genuinely couldn't

00:05:17.989 --> 00:05:21.158
collude, not two family members who live together.

00:05:21.158 --> 00:05:23.519
Second, set up the vault as a two of

00:05:23.519 --> 00:05:27.603
three, using an established, audited multisig contract; Safe is

00:05:27.603 --> 00:05:30.392
the most widely used one in this space.

00:05:30.392 --> 00:05:33.261
Third, physically distribute a hardware device

00:05:33.261 --> 00:05:35.637
to each key holder's own location.

00:05:35.637 --> 00:05:38.330
And fourth, test it first with a small amount,

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:40.737
before you ever move real savings into it.

00:05:42.037 --> 00:05:45.068
One illustrative way to see why this matters.

00:05:45.068 --> 00:05:47.560
A single key gives an attacker exactly

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:50.288
one target, and one signature ends it.

00:05:50.288 --> 00:05:52.594
A two of three vault, keys genuinely

00:05:52.594 --> 00:05:55.648
apart, gives an attacker three separate targets,

00:05:55.648 --> 00:05:57.818
none of which is enough alone.

00:05:57.818 --> 00:05:59.923
The defence isn't a bigger lock.

00:05:59.923 --> 00:06:01.848
It's removing the one moment where

00:06:01.848 --> 00:06:03.443
a single “yes” is sufficient.

00:06:04.743 --> 00:06:05.833
Quick check.

00:06:05.833 --> 00:06:09.148
How does multisig actually help against a physical threat?

00:06:13.568 --> 00:06:16.305
The answer: no single person can be forced to

00:06:16.305 --> 00:06:19.756
move everything, because the other keys needed to approve

00:06:19.756 --> 00:06:23.147
the transaction are somewhere else, held by someone else.

00:06:24.446 --> 00:06:25.681
Home and devices.

00:06:26.981 --> 00:06:29.136
Don't tell people who don't need to know.

00:06:29.136 --> 00:06:30.886
Keep your hardware wallet, and any

00:06:30.886 --> 00:06:32.636
written backup, out of sight.

00:06:32.636 --> 00:06:34.921
Not in a drawer a houseguest could

00:06:34.921 --> 00:06:38.447
open, and not photographed “for safekeeping.” Encrypt

00:06:38.447 --> 00:06:40.797
your devices, so a stolen laptop or

00:06:40.797 --> 00:06:43.278
phone isn't also a stolen seed phrase.

00:06:44.578 --> 00:06:47.268
Notice the pattern across this whole lesson.

00:06:47.268 --> 00:06:48.501
None of it is about a

00:06:48.501 --> 00:06:50.988
smarter contract, or a better exchange.

00:06:50.988 --> 00:06:53.817
It's entirely about how you've arranged people,

00:06:53.817 --> 00:06:56.828
places and habits, before anything goes wrong.

00:06:56.828 --> 00:06:58.738
That's what makes it work even when

00:06:58.738 --> 00:07:00.488
a low profile has already failed.

00:07:01.788 --> 00:07:04.783
So here's the practical setup, start to finish.

00:07:04.783 --> 00:07:07.238
Keep your operating wallet's balance modest

00:07:07.238 --> 00:07:09.323
and your vault address unlinked.

00:07:09.323 --> 00:07:11.139
Split custody of the vault two

00:07:11.139 --> 00:07:13.607
of three, keys in separate locations.

00:07:13.607 --> 00:07:15.817
Store the hardware wallet and any written

00:07:15.817 --> 00:07:18.537
backup out of sight, encrypted where possible.

00:07:18.537 --> 00:07:20.760
And simply don't discuss your holdings with

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:22.677
anyone who doesn't need to know.

00:07:22.677 --> 00:07:24.902
None of that requires new software.

00:07:24.902 --> 00:07:27.822
It's entirely how you structure what you already have.

00:07:29.122 --> 00:07:30.907
Last check for this lesson.

00:07:30.907 --> 00:07:32.677
Name one thing that links your

00:07:32.677 --> 00:07:34.332
identity to a wallet address.

00:07:38.632 --> 00:07:41.750
The answer: an exchange withdrawal that already

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:43.699
has your I.D. on file, a name

00:07:43.699 --> 00:07:46.817
service, or simply posting the address publicly.

00:07:48.117 --> 00:07:50.141
Before your turn, here's the checklist

00:07:50.141 --> 00:07:52.257
version of everything you just saw.

00:07:52.257 --> 00:07:54.378
Have your savings addresses never

00:07:54.378 --> 00:07:56.612
been posted or linked publicly.

00:07:56.612 --> 00:07:59.847
No holdings or wins on social media, ever.

00:07:59.847 --> 00:08:01.592
Vault keys held so no single

00:08:01.592 --> 00:08:03.997
person could move everything alone.

00:08:03.997 --> 00:08:06.339
And backups stored discreetly, somewhere a

00:08:06.339 --> 00:08:08.137
casual search wouldn't find them.

00:08:09.437 --> 00:08:11.822
Your turn, 5 to 15 minutes.

00:08:11.822 --> 00:08:14.083
Check every address you've ever posted,

00:08:14.083 --> 00:08:15.852
and stop posting new ones.

00:08:15.852 --> 00:08:18.632
Confirm your vault address has never been shared.

00:08:18.632 --> 00:08:21.027
And if you don't already have a split-custody

00:08:21.027 --> 00:08:23.265
vault, write down who would hold each key,

00:08:23.265 --> 00:08:24.827
and where, before you need it.

00:08:26.127 --> 00:08:29.489
To recap: a public ledger means privacy is something

00:08:29.489 --> 00:08:32.707
you protect, not something you get by default.

00:08:32.707 --> 00:08:35.400
Physical risk is real, and low profile

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:38.576
alone isn't enough; separated multisig is the

00:08:38.576 --> 00:08:41.292
fix that holds even under coercion.

00:08:41.292 --> 00:08:44.331
And the quiet habits at home, discretion, encryption,

00:08:44.331 --> 00:08:46.132
out of sight, back all of it up.

00:08:47.432 --> 00:08:50.017
That's privacy and physical security.

00:08:50.017 --> 00:08:53.334
Next up, Lesson 1.9: smart accounts and account

00:08:53.334 --> 00:08:57.618
abstraction, where your wallet itself gets programmable rules.
